<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sigismund III Vasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1566">1566</a>–<a href="/years/1632">1632</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-vasa">House of Vasa</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sigismund III Vasa was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and grand duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> from 1587 to 1632, and king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> from 1592 until he was deposed in 1599. A zealous Catholic, he moved the royal residence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and fought long wars with Sweden, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>; during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a> his forces occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sigismund-iii-vasa.jpg" alt="Sigismund III Vasa" width="320" height="544" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pieter Soutman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soutman_Sigismund_III_Vasa_in_coronation_robes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sigismund was born on 20 June 1566 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gripsholm-castle">Gripsholm Castle</a>, the second child and only son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-jagiellon">Catherine Jagiellon</a> and Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-of-sweden">John III of Sweden</a>. The couple had been held prisoner there since 1563, after John staged a failed rebellion against his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-xiv-of-sweden">Eric XIV of Sweden</a>. Catherine was a daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old">Sigismund I the Old</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and Sigismund was raised as a Catholic, although his younger sister Anna Vasa of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> was a Lutheran.</p><p>In 1587 he stood for election to the Polish throne after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-bathory">Stephen Báthory</a>. Queen Dowager Anna and several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> backed him as a descendant of the Jagiellons, while nobles associated with the Zborowski family opposed him. He was elected on 19 August 1587.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Chancellor Jan Zamoyski was hostile to the king almost from his arrival, criticising his liking for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> culture and his cold character. The king's friends came largely from the higher clergy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, and religious freedoms guaranteed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-confederation">Warsaw Confederation</a> of 1573 came under pressure. When mob violence against non-Catholics broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> in 1591, dissidents gathered in conventions and Sigismund had to yield to their demands. In 1606 his efforts to strengthen royal authority provoked the Zebrzydowski rebellion, an armed rising of nobles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>.</p><p>After succeeding his father in 1592 he also ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, where the Uppsala Resolution of 1594 had promised to uphold the Lutheran faith. He placed his own commanders in the castles and appointed Catholics to high office, which angered many Swedes. A civil war began in 1597 under his uncle Duke Charles, who took most of the castles. Sigismund accepted a peace at Linköping, then fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> with his sister, and Charles deposed him in 1599.</p><p>In the east he used the Russian Time of Troubles to invade from 1609, and the campaign ended with the seizure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> and the occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. A Polish–Ottoman war followed in 1620–1621 over Moldavia. In 1626 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> invaded and later annexed parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflanty-voivodeship">Polish Livonia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Towards the end of his reign Sigismund withdrew from politics and devoted himself to family matters and the performing arts. In his last days he was bedridden with gout and joint pain. His rule of nearly 45 years is seen as controversial: it brought considerable developments in architecture, the arts and culture, but also conspiracies, religious conflict and the long war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. His policies began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> there. Szymon Starowolski ranked him above his predecessors, while opponents thought he had lived too long. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-iv-vasa">Władysław IV Vasa</a> succeeded him and later commissioned the column in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> that bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sigismund III Vasa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The high point of Commonwealth arms came in 1610, when Hetman Stanisław Żółkiewski defeated a larger Russian and Swedish force at the Battle of Klushino and Polish troops occupied Moscow, where a group of boyars elected <b>Sigismund III</b>’s son Władysław, the future Władysław IV, as Tsar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the final phase of Sweden's civil war, supporters of the deposed King <b>Sigismund III Vasa</b> were publicly executed at Turku (Åbo) in Finland.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-10">10 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sigismund III Vasa</b>, king of Poland and grand duke of Lithuania since 1587, succeeded his father John III as King of Sweden.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-17">17 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Senate Hall of the Royal Castle in Warsaw, Shuisky, deposed in Russia, and his retinue paid homage to King <b>Sigismund III Vasa</b> of Poland and the teenage Prince Władysław.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-29">29 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gripsholm-castle">Gripsholm Castle</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.26,17.22&amp;year=1608" aria-label="Gripsholm Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sigismund III Vasa"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4446 5010h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4446 5010h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.26,17.22&amp;year=1608">What was here in 1608? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-of-sweden"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-iii-of-sweden.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">John III of Sweden</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; 1537–1592</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/catherine-jagiellon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/catherine-jagiellon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Catherine Jagiellon</span><span class="tp">Mother &#183; 1526–1583</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-iv-vasa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/w-adys-aw-iv-vasa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Władysław IV Vasa</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 1595–1648</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
